      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd.. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Post-pancreaticoduodenectomy hemorrhage is mostly due to the gastroduodenal artery stump erosion. The diagnosis of arterial bleeding is done by digestive endoscopy, selective angiography or video capsule endoscopy. On failure of etiological research, surgery is the last resort despite its technical difficulties."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "CASE PRESENTATION",
            "content": "A 63 years-old woman was admitted in surgery, nine months after cephalic pancreaticoduodenectomy for a pain of the right hypochondria combined with a pneumoperitoneum, after a 3rd episode of hemorrhage. Exploratory laparotomy is performed after a third hemorrhagic episode and failure of etiological research. Bleeding from the gastroduodenal artery stump was discovered and successfully treated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "DISCUSSION",
            "content": "Post-pancreaticoduodenectomy hemorrhage can occur very late. In these cases, a secondary arterial erosion obstructed by left hemi-liver should not be excluded. In these cases, despite the technical risks, surgery is required."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The failure of the means used for diagnostic must lead to the surgery right away, despite operating risk."
          }
        ]
      },
